                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "c5e4b1e7-5900-4d51-b0bb-67903f6f7cfe",
                        "source_entry_id": "50c2a5a5-850a-3905-9637-f5f8e1182f8a",
                        "source_sense_id": "9704feb4-7c13-31b9-a489-388521c2b572",
                        "target_lang_word": "confusedly",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "confusedly",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "d2680938-3669-3d6c-b0b3-6ab67f6aa179",
                                        "entry_id": "621a9213-14a5-371d-96eb-1c8b6b4ea461",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "In a confused manner."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "480a987a-2df5-42c1-a476-5be89875274c",
                                                "sense_id": "d2680938-3669-3d6c-b0b3-6ab67f6aa179",
                                                "text": "He wanted Pikes to ſet before his Archers: / Inſtead whereof, ſharpe Stakes pluckt out of Hedges / They pitched in the ground confuſedly, / To keepe the Horſemen off, from breaking in."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "143dee78-3571-47eb-a9ef-3f3d3a6c6dde",
                                                "sense_id": "d2680938-3669-3d6c-b0b3-6ab67f6aa179",
                                                "text": "Iohnson [i.e., Ben Jonson] they say 's turned Epigrammatist, / Soe think not I, believe it they that list. / Peruse his booke, thou shalt not find a dram / Of witt befitting a true Epigram. / Perhaps some scraps of play-bookes thou maist see, / Collected heer & there confusedlie, / Which piece his broken stuffe; if thou but note, / Iust like soe many patches on a cote."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "211406d2-9a0f-4306-8d94-708207abffb7",
                                                "sense_id": "d2680938-3669-3d6c-b0b3-6ab67f6aa179",
                                                "text": "Now ye have the Kingis letter and this ſupplicatioun both coppeit; read and conſidder both the ane and the uther, with the Counſallis anſuer ſent to the ſaid ſupplicatioun, quhilk is ſo ſtrangelie and confuſedlie ſet down that I can not wnderſtand the ſamen perfectlie weill."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "441b0f50-e2b6-4909-af25-ff005359ff85",
                                                "sense_id": "d2680938-3669-3d6c-b0b3-6ab67f6aa179",
                                                "text": "After the prayer, Mr. Byfield the ſcribe, reads the propoſition and the Scriptures, whereupon the Aſſemblie debates in a moſt grave and orderlie way. No man is called up to ſpeak; bot who ſtands up of his own accord, he ſpeaks ſo long as he will without interruption. If two or three ſtand up at once, then the divines confuſedlie calls on his name whom they deſyre to hear firſt: On whom the loudeſt and manieſt voices calls, he ſpeaks."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "c6fff74f-afea-45cf-b4dd-2ed4025f851a",
                                                "sense_id": "d2680938-3669-3d6c-b0b3-6ab67f6aa179",
                                                "text": "A Svveet diſorder in the dreſſe / Kindles in cloathes a vvantonneſſe: / […] / A Cuffe neglectfull, and thereby / Ribbands to flovv confuſedly: / […] / Do more bevvitch me, than vvhen Art / Is too preciſe in every part."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "7301e473-9d6e-44eb-973f-de195b9255a5",
                                                "sense_id": "d2680938-3669-3d6c-b0b3-6ab67f6aa179",
                                                "text": "The womb of Nature, and perhaps her Grave, / Of neither Sea, nor Shore, nor Air, nor Fire, / But all theſe in their pregnant cauſes mixt / Confus’dly, and which thus muſt ever fight, [...]"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "374a971b-96e3-4ab9-bb27-51a39abcef0b",
                                                "sense_id": "d2680938-3669-3d6c-b0b3-6ab67f6aa179",
                                                "text": "There were women weeping around—I hung over it, and joined my sad tears to theirs—all this time no distinct idea presented itself to my mind; but my thoughts rambled to various subjects, reflecting confusedly on my misfortunes, and their cause."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "1edecdc2-ba0c-44b9-9a5c-1efc7ebc4827",
                                                "sense_id": "d2680938-3669-3d6c-b0b3-6ab67f6aa179",
                                                "text": "She heard confusedly the busy, indifferent voices around her, and wished her mind could flow into that easy, babbling current."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "47c0b2c2-9e76-4f25-831f-6d64217ef0fd",
                                                "sense_id": "d2680938-3669-3d6c-b0b3-6ab67f6aa179",
                                                "text": "And though I have turned the talk by hook or crook / Until her praise should be the uppermost theme, / A woman spoke of some new tale she had read, / A man confusedly in a half dream / As though some other name ran in his head."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
